              "logprobs": null,
              "message": {
                "content": "## What the record shows\n\nAt 2 Fixture Ave, Brooklyn, the building was erected in 1930, which places it before January 1, 1960. The building has 8 residential units. The public record does not tell whether a child currently resides in your unit.\n\n## What the law says\n\n**NYC Administrative Code § 27-2056.4** requires that in buildings erected before January 1, 1960 (which includes this one), if a child of applicable age resides in a dwelling unit, the owner **must investigate for peeling paint at least once a year**, and more often if necessary—such as when a tenant makes a complaint about a paint condition that could cause a lead-based paint hazard, or the Department of Health issues a notice of violation.\n\n**NYC Administrative Code § 27-2056.6** states: \"The existence of lead-based paint in any dwelling unit in a multiple dwelling where a child of applicable age resides shall constitute a class C immediately hazardous violation if such paint is peeling or is on a deteriorated subsurface.\"\n\nClass C violations are the most serious category HPD uses; they are classified as \"immediately hazardous.\"\n\n## What this might mean (HouseCheck's inference, not legal advice)\n\nPeeling paint in a pre-1960 building where a child lives is treated by law as a lead-paint hazard. If the peeling paint contains lead, the law treats it as an immediately hazardous condition. Whether the paint contains lead requires testing by a certified inspector using an x-ray fluorescence analyzer.\n\nThe owner's duty to investigate depends on knowing or being told that a child lives in the unit. If you have not told the landlord, the law still requires investigation if the owner knows or should reasonably know of a peeling-paint condition, and it requires investigation upon a tenant's complaint.\n\n## A question to ask a legal professional\n\n\"I have a young child in my apartment at [building address], which was built in 1930. There is peeling paint on [surfaces/areas]. The landlord has not investigated or repaired it since I reported it on [date]. Under NYC Administrative Code § 27-2056.6, does this peeling paint constitute a lead-paint hazard requiring immediate repair, and what are my options if the landlord does not respond?\"\n\nCall one of these free services today:\n\n- **Housing Court Answers**: 212-962-4795 (Monday–Friday 9 am–5 pm) — Information about Housing Court and referrals to free legal help.\n- **Met Council on Housing — Tenants Rights Hotline**: 212-979-0611 (Monday & Wednesday 1:30 pm–8 pm, Friday 1:30 pm–5 pm) — Brief answers and free referrals.\n- **The Legal Aid Society — Housing**: Brooklyn 718-722-3100 — Free legal assistance if you qualify.\n\n**Document this** while you have it:\n- Dated photographs of the peeling paint\n- Every written message you sent to the landlord about it, with the date sent\n- Your response to any lead-paint notice the landlord sent you\n- If you file a complaint through 311, save your SR (service request) number.",
